              "inline_data": null,
              "text": "To understand what opioids are derived from, it is helpful to look at how they are made. **\"Opioid\"** is an umbrella term for a broad class of drugs that reduce pain by acting on the body's opioid receptors. Depending on the specific drug, they can be derived directly from a plant, modified in a lab, or entirely man-made.\n\nOpioids are categorized into three main groups based on how they are derived:\n\n---\n\n### 1. Natural Opioids (Opiates)\nThese are derived directly from nature\u2014specifically from the **opium poppy plant (*Papaver somniferum*)**. \n* **How they are derived:** When the seed pods of the opium poppy are scraped, they secrete a milky latex (sap). This sap contains naturally occurring chemical compounds called alkaloids, which are harvested and refined.\n* **Examples:** \n  * **Morphine** (the primary active pain-relieving compound in the poppy)\n  * **Codeine**\n  * **Thebaine** (a natural compound with no pain-relief properties of its own, but heavily used as a starter material to make other drugs)\n\n### 2. Semi-Synthetic Opioids\nThese are a hybrid of nature and chemistry. They cannot be harvested directly from the plant, but they cannot be made entirely from scratch either.\n* **How they are derived:** Scientists take natural alkaloids extracted from the opium poppy (most commonly morphine or thebaine) and chemically alter their molecular structures in a laboratory to create a new, often more potent, drug.\n* **Examples:**\n  * **Oxycodone** (OxyContin, Percocet) \u2013 synthesized from thebaine\n  * **Hydrocodone** (Vicodin)\n  * **Heroin** (diacetylmorphine) \u2013 synthesized by chemically processing morphine\n  * **Buprenorphine** (Suboxone)\n\n### 3. Synthetic Opioids\nThese are entirely artificial and have no direct botanical origin.\n* **How they are derived:** They are synthesized entirely in chemical laboratories from basic chemical precursors. Although they do not contain any part of the poppy plant, they are engineered to mimic the exact molecular shape of natural opiates, allowing them to bind to the same pain-relieving receptors in the brain.\n* **Examples:**\n  * **Fentanyl** (an extremely potent painkiller)\n  * **Methadone**\n  * **Tramadol**\n\n---\n\n### Summary: \"Opiate\" vs. \"Opioid\"\nWhile people often use the terms interchangeably, there is a historical distinction:\n* **Opiates** strictly refer only to the natural compounds derived directly from the poppy plant (like morphine and codeine).\n* **Opioids** is the broader term that includes everything\u2014natural opiates, semi-synthetics, and fully synthetic lab-made drugs.",
              "thought": null,
